2 Data Partitioning and Sharding Strategies
With more than 4 million accounts generating heavy transactional volume, a single database instance will eventually face storage and write throughput bottlenecks. ApexPay resolves this through Horizontal Database Sharding.
Sharding Key Evaluation
Rejected Alternative: Sharding by Timestamp: Causes write-hotspotting, where all incoming writes hit the single current partition while historical partitions sit idle.
Selected Strategy: Hybrid Hash-Range Sharding on customer_id:
Routing Logic: The shard router applies a cryptographic hash function to the customer_id: Shard_ID = Hash(customer_id) Modulo Total_Shards.
Locality Benefit: All accounts, balances, and outgoing transfers for a specific customer reside on the same physical shard. This ensures that typical account lookups and balance updates run locally without distributed network hops.
Handling Cross-Shard Distributed Transfers
When a transfer sends funds from an account on Shard A to an account on Shard B, the system uses a Two-Phase Commit (2PC) protocol managed by a distributed transaction coordinator:
Prepare Phase: The coordinator writes a global prepare message and instructs both shards to lock the relevant rows, verify available balances, and write changes to their local transaction logs. Both shards confirm readiness (Vote_Commit).
Commit Phase: Once all participating shards vote to proceed, the coordinator writes a permanent commit record and orders both shards to apply their balance changes and release locks. If either shard fails to prepare, all shards roll back.

